WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.100
One red photon carries more energy than the climb from water to NADP+ plus seems to

2
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:11.380
require. So why does a chloroplast spend two photons on every electron?

3
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.480
Track one electron here.

4
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.560
More negative potential is uphill.

5
00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:24.060
Water starts at about plus 0 .82 volts.

6
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.740
And a dP plus weights at about -0.32 V.

7
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:36.553
The gap is 1.14 V. A red photon carries about 1.8 eV.

8
00:00:37.793 --> 00:00:40.053
On paper, one photon looks sufficient.

9
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:45.433
But it must arrive somewhere stable, not merely visit an excited state.

10
00:00:47.053 --> 00:00:51.093
Predict: how many photons does each electron actually need, one or two?

11
00:00:56.717 --> 00:01:03.397
Two. Fast downhill steps pull separated charges apart before they recombine.

12
00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:06.857
Those steps spend much of the photon's energy.

13
00:01:08.237 --> 00:01:12.837
Each stable lift keeps only about 0.7 to 0.8 V.

14
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.960
Now place that rule into the thylakoid membrane.

15
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.660
Photosystem II takes the first photon, sending one electron into a downhill chain.

16
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.780
It then replaces that missing electron from water.

17
00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:33.753
Splitting water releases oxygen, while protons enter the lumen.

18
00:01:35.213 --> 00:01:40.053
Cytochrome b6f uses the downhill bridge to add more lumen protons.

19
00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:43.393
Together, those protons build a gradient.

20
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:49.167
At Photosystem I, a second photon lifts the same electron again.

21
00:01:50.547 --> 00:01:53.207
Downhill steps stabilize that charge separation too.

22
00:01:54.727 --> 00:02:00.747
Ferredoxin and FNR deliver electrons to NADP+, +, making NADPH.

23
00:02:01.867 --> 00:02:03.627
That is the Z-scheme's logic.

24
00:02:04.887 --> 00:02:09.687
Two light-driven lifts in series span a gap no single stable lift covers.

25
00:02:10.987 --> 00:02:13.647
The electron climbs, falls, then climbs again.

26
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.560
Repeat the route four times to release one oxygen molecule.

27
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.800
Linear flow uses 8 photons and makes 2 NADPH.

28
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:30.500
Water and cytochrome b6f together place about 12 protons in the lumen.

29
00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:37.503
As protons flow back to the stroma, ATP synthase uses their gradient to make ATP.

30
00:02:38.903 --> 00:02:41.343
This is linear flow, the simplest form.

31
00:02:42.743 --> 00:02:46.743
Chloroplasts can also cycle electrons to adjust ATP output.

32
00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:49.653
Return to the opening puzzle.

33
00:02:50.993 --> 00:02:55.293
A photon has enough raw energy, but a reaction center cannot keep it all.

34
00:02:56.593 --> 00:03:01.953
Two stable lifts turn water's electrons into NADPH and a proton gradient.
